## 🛠️ Step-by-step commands

### 1) Update package lists
```bash
sudo apt update
```
*Fetch latest package index from repositories.* 

### 2) Install John the Ripper
```bash
sudo apt install -y john
```
*Install John the Ripper tool.

---

### 3) Decompress rockyou (if gzipped) — go to wordlists folder
```bash
cd /usr/share/wordlists
ls
sudo gunzip rockyou.txt.gz
ls
```
*Go to the wordlists folder, list contents, and decompress `rockyou.txt.gz` (Kali default).

### 4) Go back to home and create a small demo wordlist
```bash
cd ~
head -n 1000 /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt > ~/rockyou-1000.txt
ls
wc -l ~/rockyou-1000.txt
```
*Create a small 1000-line subset and verify line count.*

### 5) Create a raw-MD5 hash of the demo password
```bash
echo -n "password123" | md5sum | awk '{print $1}' > ~/john-hashes.txt
ls
```
*Generate the MD5 hash of `password123` and save it into `john-hashes.txt`.*

### 6) Check the hash
```bash
cat ~/john-hashes.txt
```
*Display the hash we will try to crack.* 👀

### 7) (Ensure password presence) Append password to small wordlist
```bash
echo "password123" >> ~/rockyou-1000.txt
```
*Make sure the demo password exists in the wordlist so the cracking demo finishes quickly.

### 8) Run John with the small wordlist (raw-MD5)
```bash
john --format=raw-md5 --wordlist=~/rockyou-1000.txt ~/john-hashes.txt
```
*John will try passwords from the small wordlist against the raw-MD5 hash.

### 9) Show cracked results (use format to be safe)
```bash
john --show --format=raw-md5 ~/john-hashes.txt
```
*Display cracked passwords (what John recovered) in a readable way.

### 10) Run John with rules (smart mutations)
```bash
john --format=raw-md5 --wordlist=~/rockyou-1000.txt --rules ~/john-hashes.txt
```
*Use built-in rules to try variations (adds case changes, suffixes, etc.) — useful when base wordlist misses exact password.* 🔁

